WEBVTT

00:00:00.127 --> 00:00:01.579
What is electric current?

00:00:02.480 --> 00:00:04.477
The rate of flow of electric charge.

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:07.150
It is measured in amperes or amps.

00:00:07.583 --> 00:00:10.895
In our simple cell circuit,
a complete conducting path is

00:00:10.895 --> 00:00:12.546
needed for a sustained current.

00:00:13.318 --> 00:00:14.110
Exactly.

00:00:14.418 --> 00:00:18.960
The cell supplies energy, not a fresh
set of electrons that vanish in the lamp.

00:00:19.528 --> 00:00:22.336
Charge carriers are already
present in the conductors.

00:00:22.857 --> 00:00:25.841
When the circuit changes,
electrical effects propagate

00:00:25.844 --> 00:00:28.352
rapidly but not infinitely fast.

00:00:29.114 --> 00:00:31.264
So current is not used up by the bulb.

00:00:31.658 --> 00:00:35.356
Energy is transferred there, for
example, to light and heating.

00:00:36.171 --> 00:00:36.716
Correct.

00:00:37.049 --> 00:00:39.412
Copper is a good electrical conductor.

00:00:39.632 --> 00:00:43.460
Plastic insulation resists current
under normal operating conditions.

00:00:43.774 --> 00:00:46.428
Graphite conducts too,
despite not being a metal.

00:00:46.875 --> 00:00:50.438
Wet materials and damaged insulation
change the safety picture.

00:00:51.245 --> 00:00:55.751
Conventional current travels from positive
to negative through the external circuit.

00:00:56.170 --> 00:01:00.452
Electron drift in a metal is in the
opposite direction, negative to positive.

00:01:01.227 --> 00:01:01.620
Good.

00:01:01.962 --> 00:01:03.428
Keep those labels separate.

00:01:03.827 --> 00:01:07.637
Conventional current is a direction
convention, not a claim that

00:01:07.646 --> 00:01:09.423
electrons have positive charge.

00:01:09.881 --> 00:01:13.588
Other conducting materials can have
other kinds of moving charge carriers.

00:01:14.386 --> 00:01:16.797
Is voltage just another word for energy?

00:01:17.521 --> 00:01:17.896
No.

00:01:18.318 --> 00:01:22.961
Potential difference is energy transferred
per unit charge measured in volts.

00:01:23.390 --> 00:01:25.987
One volt means one joule per coulomb.

00:01:26.434 --> 00:01:29.934
The water pump analogy can help,
but voltage is not literally a

00:01:29.934 --> 00:01:32.130
fluid pressure or the energy itself.

00:01:32.911 --> 00:01:37.693
Current in amps, voltage in volts,
charge in coulombs, energy in joules.

00:01:38.238 --> 00:01:41.523
My imaginary exam mark is
staying firmly in its seat.

00:01:41.863 --> 00:01:45.542
Circuit diagrams use standard symbols
rather than realistic pictures.

00:01:46.131 --> 00:01:49.448
The long cell line is positive
and the short line negative.

00:01:50.021 --> 00:01:51.830
Several cells can form a battery.

00:01:52.281 --> 00:01:54.553
The circle with a cross represents a lamp.

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:59.127
A rectangle represents a resistor
in our convention, with an arrow

00:01:59.137 --> 00:02:00.522
for an adjustable resistor.

00:02:00.954 --> 00:02:03.054
A circle with A is an ammeter.

00:02:03.388 --> 00:02:04.768
V is a voltmeter.

00:02:05.152 --> 00:02:06.298
M is a motor.

00:02:07.130 --> 00:02:07.676
Correct.

00:02:07.997 --> 00:02:10.265
A solid dot marks a connected junction.

00:02:10.624 --> 00:02:13.994
Crossing lines without a junction
need clear drawing so nobody

00:02:13.994 --> 00:02:15.567
invents an accidental connection.

00:02:16.045 --> 00:02:18.491
Use the symbol set
expected by your course.

00:02:19.271 --> 00:02:21.823
A diode mainly conducts in one direction.

00:02:22.309 --> 00:02:26.500
An LED is a light-emitting diode
shown with outward light arrows.

00:02:26.889 --> 00:02:29.990
It needs correct polarity and
suitable current limiting.

00:02:30.755 --> 00:02:31.547
Exactly.

00:02:31.836 --> 00:02:35.348
A fuse melts and opens a circuit
if excessive current persists

00:02:35.348 --> 00:02:36.757
under its rated conditions.

00:02:37.119 --> 00:02:40.724
It is not a reusable switch or
permission to exceed equipment ratings.

00:02:41.126 --> 00:02:44.186
Buzzers and motors also need
suitable rated supplies.

00:02:44.955 --> 00:02:47.147
Where do the ammeter and voltmeter go?

00:02:48.028 --> 00:02:51.707
Ammeter in series, so the current
being measured passes through it.

00:02:52.095 --> 00:02:56.310
Voltmeter in parallel across the component
to measure its potential difference.

00:02:57.110 --> 00:02:57.735
Perfect.

00:02:58.280 --> 00:03:02.043
Never place an ammeter directly across
a cell as if it were a voltmeter.

00:03:02.470 --> 00:03:05.121
Its low resistance can
allow excessive current.

00:03:05.473 --> 00:03:07.499
An exam mix-up can lose marks.

00:03:07.777 --> 00:03:09.820
A real mix-up can damage equipment

00:03:10.184 --> 00:03:14.481
Two equal resistors are in series
across a six-volt ideal supply.

00:03:14.786 --> 00:03:17.344
What can we say about current
and potential difference?

00:03:18.163 --> 00:03:20.639
Current is the same
everywhere in the single loop.

00:03:21.077 --> 00:03:24.239
Each resistor has three volts
across it, and the potential

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:26.174
differences add to six volts.

00:03:27.072 --> 00:03:27.629
Correct.

00:03:28.177 --> 00:03:31.311
Equal sharing requires
equal relevant resistances.

00:03:31.731 --> 00:03:35.377
With unequal components, the
voltage split need not be equal.

00:03:35.924 --> 00:03:40.122
Adding an ordinary lamp in series to
a fixed supply generally increases

00:03:40.122 --> 00:03:41.932
resistance and reduces current.

00:03:42.732 --> 00:03:46.754
The lamps usually become dimmer, and
an open break anywhere in that single

00:03:46.755 --> 00:03:48.747
path stops their sustained current.

00:03:49.238 --> 00:03:52.739
But current was not being gradually
eaten by each lamp beforehand.

00:03:53.498 --> 00:03:57.203
Now connect separate branches
across the same supply terminals.

00:03:57.687 --> 00:04:00.127
What is shared and what stays the same?

00:04:00.986 --> 00:04:04.109
Each branch has the same potential
difference across its ends.

00:04:04.519 --> 00:04:08.219
Total current divides at the junction
and equals the sum of branch currents.

00:04:08.659 --> 00:04:10.676
It does not necessarily split equally.

00:04:11.396 --> 00:04:12.182
Exactly.

00:04:12.481 --> 00:04:14.764
Suppose one branch takes 0.2

00:04:14.767 --> 00:04:16.972
amps and another takes 0.3

00:04:16.972 --> 00:04:19.139
amps, what does the supply deliver?

00:04:19.967 --> 00:04:20.965
0.5

00:04:20.965 --> 00:04:21.409
amps.

00:04:21.934 --> 00:04:24.133
Charge conservation is
keeping the accounts.

00:04:24.584 --> 00:04:27.769
At the returning junction,
those branch currents recombine.

00:04:28.582 --> 00:04:28.950
Good.

00:04:29.263 --> 00:04:32.999
If one branch opens, another
intact branch can still work.

00:04:33.694 --> 00:04:36.708
Adding parallel lamps to a
sufficiently steady supply can

00:04:36.709 --> 00:04:40.000
leave their brightness similar, but
a real supply may sag or overload.
